---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import QiblaFinder from '../../components/QiblaFinder.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../../components/RelatedTools.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="Qibla Finder Online — Direction to Kaaba from My Location"
	description="Online compass for Qibla — find the direction to the Kaaba from my location. Quibla finder with great-circle bearing and distance to Mecca, computed privately in your browser."
	path="/en/qibla/"
	keywords="online compass for qibla, quibla finder online compass, online compass kabba to find north from my location, qibla direction, direction to kaaba, find qibla from my location"
>
	<PageHero
		eyebrow="qibla finder"
		title="Face the Kaaba, anywhere on Earth."
		description="The great-circle bearing to Makkah is computed on your device from your coordinates — accurate to the degree, private by design."
	>
		<QiblaFinder />
	</PageHero>

	<section class="border-t border-hairline bg-canvas">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-[1200px] grid gap-6 px-4 py-16 sm:px-6 md:grid-cols-2">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow mb-2">how it's calculated</p>
				<p class="text-sm text-body leading-6">
					The bearing uses the shortest great-circle path between your location and the Kaaba
					(21.4225° N, 39.8262° E). It accounts for Earth's curvature, so it differs from a flat
					compass-rose reading.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow mb-2">put it into practice</p>
				<p class="text-sm text-body leading-6">
					Note the bearing, then open the compass tool and turn until your heading matches it.
					Or calibrate outside away from metal for the best accuracy.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="border-t border-hairline bg-canvas">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6 md:py-24">
			<p class="eyebrow">the qibla finder</p>
			<h2 class="mt-3 text-display-lg text-ink">Find the Qibla direction from my location</h2>

			<p class="mt-6 text-body-lg text-body">
				A <strong class="text-ink font-medium">Qibla finder online</strong> tells you the exact
				direction to face for prayer — the bearing from wherever you are to the Kaaba in Mecca,
				Saudi Arabia (21.4225° N, 39.8262° E). This page computes that direction from your live
				location or from any coordinates you enter, showing both the bearing in degrees and the
				compass direction, plus the great-circle distance to Makkah. No app, no install, no
				account — open the page and know which way to pray.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				Muslims around the world pray toward the Kaaba five times a day, and getting the
				direction right matters. Whether you are travelling, new to an area, or simply want to
				confirm the direction in your home, this free <strong class="text-ink font-medium">quibla
				finder online compass</strong> gives you an accurate answer in seconds.
			</p>

			<h3 class="mt-10 text-display-sm text-ink">How the direction to the Kaaba is calculated</h3>
			<p class="mt-3 leading-7">
				The <strong class="text-ink font-medium">direction to Kaaba from my location</strong> is
				not a flat-map shortcut. Because Earth is curved, the correct bearing follows the shortest
				great-circle path between two points. The bearing to Mecca from London is roughly 118°,
				from New York about 58°, from Jakarta about 295°, and from Sydney about 295° west of
				north — numbers that surprise many people precisely because they are great-circle
				bearings, not straight-line map directions. This tool performs the same spherical
				trigonometry used in aircraft navigation, so the result is accurate to the degree wherever
				you are on the planet.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				Because the calculation runs entirely in your browser, your coordinates are never sent to
				a server. Many online Qibla tools quietly log your location; this one cannot. It is an
				online compass for Qibla that is as private as it is precise.
			</p>

			<h3 class="mt-10 text-display-sm text-ink">Putting the Qibla bearing into practice</h3>
			<p class="mt-3 leading-7">
				Once you have the bearing, the next step is turning it into a direction you can physically
				face. The simplest method: note the bearing in degrees, open the
				<a href="/" class="text-link underline decoration-link/30 hover:decoration-link">live online compass</a>,
				and turn until your heading matches the Qibla bearing. The compass dial shows your
				heading in real time, so matching it takes seconds.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				For the best result, tap "Use location" in both tools so declination is corrected and the
				readings are true-north based. If you do not have a compass handy, you can also orient
				yourself with the sun: in the northern hemisphere the sun sits due south at local noon, in
				the southern hemisphere due north — a reliable daytime reference point for working out
				your general orientation before fine-tuning with the bearing.
			</p>

			<div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
				<div class="card-soft p-5">
					<h4 class="text-sm font-medium text-ink mb-1">Common search: "online compass kabba to find north from my location"</h4>
					<p class="text-sm leading-6">
						That phrase is exactly what this tool answers. Locate yourself, read the Qibla
						bearing, then follow it on the compass — a free online compass for Qibla from any
						location on Earth.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card-soft p-5">
					<h4 class="text-sm font-medium text-ink mb-1">Distance to Makkah</h4>
					<p class="text-sm leading-6">
						Alongside the bearing you get the great-circle distance to the Kaaba — helpful for
						planning travel and understanding how the bearing changes with distance.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<h3 class="mt-10 text-display-sm text-ink">Qibla direction that changes with your location</h3>
			<p class="mt-3 leading-7">
				Your <strong class="text-ink font-medium">qibla direction</strong> depends entirely on
				where you are. It changes from city to city and even street to street, so a value you
				looked up years ago in one country is rarely right in another. That is why a live
				calculator is the dependable choice: it computes the bearing fresh for your current
				coordinates every time. Travel a few hundred kilometres and the bearing can shift several
				degrees — and this page keeps up automatically.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-4 leading-7">
				The tool works with manual coordinates too, which is handy when you want to check the
				direction for a place you are about to visit, or when your browser permission is off.
				Enter a city or coordinates and the Qibla finder returns the same accurate bearing and
				distance. Wherever the question is "find qibla from my location", this page answers it —
				privately, precisely, and for free.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<RelatedTools current="/qibla/" />
</Layout>